#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "2aca2611-5903-5a92-8409-8fe3af32614a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5827" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(2x+1)(x-2)(x-7) > 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2x+1)(x-2)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) > 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $x=2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n\\left( -\\infty,-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right), \\quad \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2},2 \\right), \\quad (2,7), \\quad (7, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\dfrac12\\right)$ | $\\left(-\\dfrac12,2\\right)$ | $(2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-7$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)> 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $\\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right)$ and $(7,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) > 0$ for all $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right) \\cup (7,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "2cdfee85-e9c7-5e91-bbef-16daaaaf1d57" :question/math-academy-id "e-5828" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x^4 + 2x^3 - 3x^2 \\leq  0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-x-3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+3)-(x+3)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+3)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2(x+3)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n( -\\infty,-3 ), \\quad ( -3,0 ), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\leq  0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-3, 0)$ and $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) \\leq  0$ is\n\n$$\nx \\in [-3,0]\\cup [0,1] =  [-3,1].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"}] :question/id #uuid "bad47d1a-817e-529d-8eb5-7a2ab446370a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5835" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x(2x^2-3) < 5x.$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2{x}^{2}-3) & <5x \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-3)-5x & <0 \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-3-5) & <0 \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-8) & <0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-4) & <0 \\\\ 2x(x+2)(x-2) & <0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) < 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-2,$ $x = 0,$ and $x = 2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad  (-2,0),\\quad (0,2),\\quad (2,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "cc631d97-d7f8-5798-8dff-f816e63bbf44" :question/math-academy-id "e-7755" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x(x+2)^2(x-1) \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)^2(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+2)^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(0,1)$ only.\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\leq 0$ for all $x \\in \\{-2\\} \\cup[0, 1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebf97375-155f-5ca3-9c47-31252ec68ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-6)\\cup (-1,5)\\cup (9,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281462} {:answer/id #uuid "54a51d7e-7dd7-5a26-8c09-ef81910ec347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-6,-1)\\cup (5,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281463} {:answer/id #uuid "02f13230-087c-5fbf-b398-f812f5e24840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-6,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281464} {:answer/id #uuid "dce204ce-bd3e-50b0-86f8-d9f8e245416a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-6)\\cup (9,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281465} {:answer/id #uuid "213a1221-c949-5a9e-9429-92f4c4827e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (5,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54a51d7e-7dd7-5a26-8c09-ef81910ec347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-6,-1)\\cup (5,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281463} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a45e54-82a7-5d32-9223-26e9967ec8eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af9edba9-c44e-54d3-b14b-8e3ddd2c39ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-116590" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x+1)(x+6)(x-5)(9-x)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)(x+6)(x-5)(9-x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-6,$ $x=-1,$ $x=5$ and $x=9.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-6),\\,(-6,-1),\\,(-1,5),\\,(5,9),\\,(9,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,-1)$ | $(-1,5)$ | $(5,9)$ | $(9,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $9-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-6,-1)$ and $(5,9).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-6,-1)\\cup (5,9).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d63501-2c71-5ff3-ad4b-0d07216805cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281469} {:answer/id #uuid "39919d7b-8428-5fb7-b9f2-0e07b8ff6d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281470} {:answer/id #uuid "e33d2fb3-33da-5375-b5ad-524e3ab24ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281471} {:answer/id #uuid "70609532-27b2-597b-a028-e55e62887a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281472} {:answer/id #uuid "7a67d2cc-4c07-5c73-a185-182e2c65bf6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39919d7b-8428-5fb7-b9f2-0e07b8ff6d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281470} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c66dc2-34d2-5195-a2f1-d831e864530b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c253474c-d011-5341-9695-96e00a1c311b" :question/math-academy-id "q-116595" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-2),\\,(-2,7),\\,(7,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-2)$ | $(-2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(x+2)^{4}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-7$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(7,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e98a5678-6111-596a-93a8-62fe1465286a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {0}\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281476} {:answer/id #uuid "ae141b53-8e11-5fd1-8a03-6c5588f83784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-7,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281477} {:answer/id #uuid "9de9e2d2-5390-5211-b0ff-9a675f812d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281478} {:answer/id #uuid "d412c8f5-eee9-5ccf-b383-fd78e60c2c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281479} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a6f50b-47b0-5012-8f98-2cad9a252dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d412c8f5-eee9-5ccf-b383-fd78e60c2c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281479} :answer-field/id #uuid "738ed013-6361-5810-819c-4fd1391228f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f53c4802-517b-5a9e-91a4-0f3a31ddb623" :question/math-academy-id "q-116614" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+7x-4x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+7)-4(x+7)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-7,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52b32ff-1ef7-5a38-bb83-77624d9f3979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281483} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ec0a51-93c2-5baa-bcd1-d39725d9fc01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281484} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf4a545-f3c3-57f0-8e89-805e05ea1591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281485} {:answer/id #uuid "3adea6e2-39b9-58e9-ae47-4789ddd3a5b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,1)\\cup (1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281486} {:answer/id #uuid "63e49d70-8d98-53f2-b360-b521bb8180dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8ec0a51-93c2-5baa-bcd1-d39725d9fc01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281484} :answer-field/id #uuid "5322fcbf-0af7-50ec-bef5-11da687b4cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6943d66-bd59-5d44-a697-d43871adf667" :question/math-academy-id "q-116617" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}-{x}^{3}>16-16x.$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & >16-16x \\\\ ({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})-(16-16x) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-x)-16(1-x) & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-16)(1-x) & >0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-4)(1-x) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+4)(x-4)(1-x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4,$ $x=1$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,1),\\,(1,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(1,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (1,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a649fe3-d5a5-538b-9486-6776577a2765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281490} {:answer/id #uuid "ea865365-ccd4-5025-8ca7-4c6d79e95681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup {0}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281491} {:answer/id #uuid "7af31f6c-3774-5ae8-9124-e4bafdccefc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [2,7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281492} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f34431-d56c-5e45-8f74-d08b9489ff27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281493} {:answer/id #uuid "dc39290d-bf47-51a0-9f19-0bdf901592bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea865365-ccd4-5025-8ca7-4c6d79e95681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup {0}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a18dca9c-5e3a-52ba-85be-4cb5824556a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1c4e102d-7322-54fc-92ee-3b46bc062dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-116695" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}-7{x}^{2}\\ge 7{x}^{2}+5{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-7{x}^{2} & \\ge 7{x}^{2}+5{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{4}-7{x}^{2}-7{x}^{2}-5{x}^{3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-5x-14) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(x-7) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+2)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,7),\\,(7,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,7)$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-7$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(7,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all  $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup {0}\\cup [7,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6298571-17b7-5765-ae0c-aa55e392b979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281497} {:answer/id #uuid "80d732d0-d7d9-512e-84d8-643ace5a0de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6298571-17b7-5765-ae0c-aa55e392b979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281497} :answer-field/id #uuid "64b512c2-2dfe-55a1-a454-202d69440e4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answe