e factor the quadratic on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =(x-3)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-3$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]⋃[3,[\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65709c4f-c2ea-55ce-9076-ecba011cdde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281544} {:answer/id #uuid "57b456a4-1bcd-589c-a7cc-2b72721b7925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b456a4-1bcd-589c-a7cc-2b72721b7925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281545} :answer-field/id #uuid "36eec41c-933f-50be-a253-1cc622990d47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9d7d4a5-f952-54c8-a2c1-6f7889109c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9d7d4a5-f952-54c8-a2c1-6f7889109c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281547} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e5c637-6f07-54c1-a07e-6fb52786d381" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327b38b0-ee8e-5b2c-9e49-d3d7d0567360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "327b38b0-ee8e-5b2c-9e49-d3d7d0567360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281549} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbff4c1b-c117-53a3-9711-e2447fc65cd8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "782f2532-11d3-5a21-a197-6419a23b812b" :question/math-academy-id "q-281748" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $10x-5{x}^{3}\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$[$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃\\,[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-5{x}^{3} & =5x(2-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5x(\\sqrt{2}-x)(\\sqrt{2}+x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=5x(\\sqrt{2}-x)(\\sqrt{2}+x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ $x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2}),\\,(-\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\sqrt{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})$ | $(-\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(0,\\sqrt{2})$ | $(\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $\\sqrt{2}-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $\\sqrt{2}+x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-\\sqrt{2},0]⋃[\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f51d12d6-dc6e-5b19-a8a6-a9cc18be1f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281552} {:answer/id #uuid "b780761f-01a2-5ee9-8947-c276302e7710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51d12d6-dc6e-5b19-a8a6-a9cc18be1f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281552} :answer-field/id #uuid "305d26f4-c969-570c-aea2-11c8c1776e44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281551} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "435208bc-c164-504e-b364-8d8a2e0b6f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "435208bc-c164-504e-b364-8d8a2e0b6f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281555} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd1ec00-1bf0-5158-9354-69fa65eeff0f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b74ab5b-c68c-5ade-b5c2-ac37db3f9080" :question/math-academy-id "q-282519" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}+16x+64\\le 2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}⋃\\,[$\n{{field-2}}$,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+16x+64 & \\le 2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}+16x+64-2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ -{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+16x+64 & \\le 0 \\\\ (-{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})+(16x+64) & \\le 0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}(x+4)+16(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (-{x}^{2}+16)(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (16-{x}^{2})(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (4-x)(4+x)(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (4-x)(4+x)^{2} & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(4-x)(4+x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into 3 subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(4+x)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative only on the interval $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in {-4}⋃[4,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "348ec929-8074-5685-b0a2-3bbb013ad125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281558} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0abced-e269-5473-b774-8c098cc1c5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0abced-e269-5473-b774-8c098cc1c5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281559} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c978f6-1ad8-5832-8f0e-9f3817990525" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281557} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8696f310-4f07-5d21-b610-8a8f8b7a39c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8696f310-4f07-5d21-b610-8a8f8b7a39c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281561} :answer-field/id #uuid "24704183-63e6-5b1b-b1f5-0115ecbb2bcf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281560} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da9a5bb-e5a6-511e-a4cd-dbd69d70a43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1da9a5bb-e5a6-511e-a4cd-dbd69d70a43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281563} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae7f98f-808f-51de-b6e2-d2800a5c4bad" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6310fb-59c4-5474-a8c4-65b3cc26c3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b6310fb-59c4-5474-a8c4-65b3cc26c3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281565} :answer-field/id #uuid "88502cf6-e3f8-59eb-a7d2-73a87402230e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b74a5db9-3027-5ac1-a642-488b8ace7eb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-282526" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{6}-3{x}^{4}\\ge {x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}.$\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$]⋃\\,{$\n{{field-2}}$}⋃\\,[$\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6}-3{x}^{4} & \\ge {x}^{5}+3{x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{6}-3{x}^{4}-{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{6}-{x}^{5}-6{x}^{4} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({x}^{2}-x-6) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}(x+2)(x-3) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{4}(x+2)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all  $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]⋃{0}⋃[3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29fd8020-1707-5d47-98dc-5f31957b091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281568} {:answer/id #uuid "cc085fe0-b011-5ecb-8e37-20696e72cec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281569} {:answer/id #uuid "de079cb3-952c-53ca-82e1-8caa580aa931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,-\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281570} {:answer/id #uuid "36099567-79ab-5f0f-b2e8-2634e337a09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-\\frac{3}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281571} {:answer/id #uuid "546e0d17-0635-56d7-99d4-455b1d4d313e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (2,\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de079cb3-952c-53ca-82e1-8caa580aa931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,-\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281570} :answer-field/id #uuid "02642322-6caf-5f8e-aeb6-c2e3871f7809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf61b99e-e4da-5fea-98ff-bb2faa24513c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40220" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x+4)(2x+3)(x-2)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+4)(2x+3)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4,$ $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,-\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{3}{2},2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,-\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{3}{2},2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x+3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-4,-\\frac{3}{2})$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-4,-\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de861d81-b017-5f34-9dba-3b25f0c7159a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281575} {:answer/id #uuid "b36314cb-2704-