 | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d27313b-580f-50d6-82df-023fe3492322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131697} {:answer/id #uuid "c095e2e3-b320-568a-99b8-c49fc2ac8f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131698} {:answer/id #uuid "c221ba55-0d66-5df0-8584-7b3cf4a3fa82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131699} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfbf121-2ec5-5587-9459-3771dff4d7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131700} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5e9fb4-c2a4-5188-9803-997171ad889f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d27313b-580f-50d6-82df-023fe3492322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131697} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccdafc97-7691-5a17-9c10-c7b1794cc870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7c7012e-7230-5abb-8c48-85a5afa58965" :question/math-academy-id "q-40367" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}-1<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(x+1)(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)(x-1)({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(-1,1)$ only.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b80e7c7-981c-54d7-85a9-dcbe661f414f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281582} {:answer/id #uuid "d345aa87-060b-5609-b2d0-e1567c76605a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (-3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281583} {:answer/id #uuid "254940a3-1509-5ce5-9e7e-46d18e77ac71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281584} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3539e6-1279-509f-a28c-58cf5e225fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281585} {:answer/id #uuid "f0719e13-1cb2-5be6-a248-b5cf35be0c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-5,-3)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d345aa87-060b-5609-b2d0-e1567c76605a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (-3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281583} :answer-field/id #uuid "7132e214-42ef-5dc6-9bc3-dde16d411338" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51403d1b-be66-52dd-8302-015edd859cd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-40592" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x+5)(x+3)(x-2)<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+5)(x+3)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-3),\\,(-3,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(-3,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (-3,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5145a445-b158-5ea8-9eef-baa43522bd38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,0]\\cup [1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281589} {:answer/id #uuid "6946dac9-35de-5f51-94df-ed5bbc13eecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281590} {:answer/id #uuid "4a75c632-d35d-5985-8281-380d8738c4d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281591} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb422bd-f2dc-564a-9e15-61631da739a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281592} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc0a8b8-5173-5c44-beb6-112a74c25e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cc0a8b8-5173-5c44-beb6-112a74c25e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281593} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c3c4baf-74d6-57c2-96f7-239ded311b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80fbb1fd-28d6-53d5-8b5b-43217e47e186" :question/math-academy-id "q-40637" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x-15) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-3x+5x-15) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x-3)+5(x-3)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+5)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+5)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-5,0)$ and $(0,3).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-5,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b8954b-43eb-54c3-9bb3-bd5faa9e2ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281596} {:answer/id #uuid "738585eb-0a09-5c00-bc6c-5e3c88d69d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281597} {:answer/id #uuid "8d68029c-10be-5c4d-899b-bdc2e0488966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281598} {:answer/id #uuid "141da4b8-05f0-5994-9d33-3f6d801664d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281599} {:answer/id #uuid "3c4c5948-1b0b-50b6-8fa6-5fd733e5d731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]\\cup [1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "738585eb-0a09-5c00-bc6c-5e3c88d69d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281597} :answer-field/id #uuid "363e82f4-a40d-576b-8175-fa25915e17c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0648491e-963d-54f5-812f-6def57c885a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-90485" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x-5)(1-2x)(x+1)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-5)(1-2x)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are  $x=-1,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $1-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},5).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},5].$"}]}
